arXiv:2509.13067v1 [cs.CV] 16 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xu Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuxuan Liang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiaolei Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yi Zheng
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haotian Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bin Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiangyang Xue
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">By cropping high-resolution images into local tiles and encoding them independently, High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models (HR-LVLMs) have demonstrated remarkable fine-grained visual understanding capabilities. However, this divide-and-conquer paradigm significantly increases the number of visual tokens, resulting in substantial computational and memory overhead. To better understand and address this challenge, we empirically investigate visual token utilization in HR-LVLMs and uncover three key findings: (1) the local tiles have varying importance, jointly determined by visual saliency and task relevance; (2) the CLS token in CLIP-based vision encoders exhibits a two-stage attention pattern across layers, with each stage attending to different types of visual tokens; (3) the visual tokens emphasized at different stages encode information at varying levels of granularity, playing complementary roles within LVLMs. Building on these insights, we propose HERO, a High-resolution visual tokEn eaRly drOpping framework that integrates content-adaptive token budget allocation with function-aware token selection. By accurately estimating tile-level importance and selectively retaining visual tokens with complementary roles, HERO achieves superior efficiency–accuracy trade-offs across diverse benchmarks and model scales, all in a training-free manner. This study provides both empirical insights and practical solutions toward efficient inference in HR-LVLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Institute of Big Data, Fudan University</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>College of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence, Fudan University</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">xu_li23@m.fudan.edu.cn, yxliang25@m.fudan.edu.cn, chenxl23@m.fudan.edu.cn, 232102404707@m.fudan.edu.cn, htchen24@m.fudan.edu.cn, libin@fudan.edu.cn, xyxue@fudan.edu.cn</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code</span> — <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_ref_self">https://github.com/Lxxx-0327/HERO</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) are a class of multimodal Large Language Models (LLMs) that can tackle various vision-language tasks through instruction following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023a</a>)</cite>. These models typically consist of three core components: a vision encoder, a vision-language adapter, and an LLM. The vision encoder extracts visual features from input images; the adapter aligns these features with the LLM’s input embedding space; and the LLM jointly interprets the aligned visual features and textual instructions to generate task-specific responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most LVLMs adopt a CLIP-pretrained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al. 2021</a>)</cite> Vision Transformer (CLIP-ViT) as the vision encoder. CLIP-ViTs offer two advantages: their serialized outputs naturally conforms to the LLM’s input format, and their image-text contrastive pretraining facilitates alignment with the LLM. However, these encoders usually operate at a fixed and limited input resolution (e.g., <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="336^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>336</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">336^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). Consequently, early LVLMs have to downscale high-resolution images to fit the constraint, leading to the loss of fine-grained visual details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome the limitation, researchers have developed High-Resolution LVLMs (HR-LVLMs), which incorporate a tile cropping strategy during image preprocessing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>. Specifically, the input image is resized and partitioned into local tiles, each matching the encoder’s input resolution. In parallel, a downsized version of the original image is generated as a global thumbnail to retain holistic context. These images are encoded independently, and their features are concatenated in sequence while entering the LLM. While this design provides high-resolution visual tokens for the LLM, it significantly increases the token count. Given the quadratic complexity of LLMs with input token length, HR-LVLMs become computationally expensive and difficult to deploy in resource-constrained scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Visual token compression has recently become a popular topic in LVLM research. Early approaches address this challenge by designing vision-language adapters with feature abstraction capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cha et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. However, these methods require model training, incurring high deployment costs and poor generalizability. Subsequent works shift toward inference-time token pruning, where initial methods remove visual tokens within the LLM based on text-to-image attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>. While these approaches offer plug-and-play flexibility, they may interfere with efficient LLM inference mechanisms such as KV caching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pope et al. 2023</a>)</cite> and Flash-Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao 2023</a>)</cite>. Therefore, more recent studies propose the concept of early dropping, which removes low-contributing visual tokens before they enter the LLM, typically based on CLS attention in the vision encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shang et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>. However, these methods are primarily designed for conventional LVLMs and do not account for the specific structure of HR-LVLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Until recently, GlobalCo<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{m}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{m}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2025a</a>)</cite> and HiRED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arif et al. 2025</a>)</cite> extend the philosophy of visual token early dropping to HR-LVLMs via a two-step pipeline. First, they allocate a constrained token budget across local tiles by estimating regional importance from the CLS attention of the global thumbnail. Next, they select a subset of visual tokens to retain within each tile according to the tile’s own CLS attention. While effective, these methods have three limitations: (1) token budget allocation overlooks the relevance between each tile and the instruction, and is further affected by spatial misalignment introduced by resizing and padding during tile cropping; (2) the choice of attention layer relies on sparse visualizations or limited ablations, lacking a systematic analysis of layerwise CLS attention dynamics; (3) token selection is applied uniformly across local tiles and the global thumbnail, ignoring their complementary roles in preserving fine-grained details and global context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we conduct a series of pilot experiments and uncover three key insights:
(1) the CLS token similarity between each tile and the global thumbnail, and the CLIP score between each tile and the instruction text, respectively reflect tile importance in terms of visual saliency and task relevance;
(2) CLS attention in CLIP-ViTs exhibits a two-stage pattern across layers, initially focusing on foreground objects and later concentrating on isolated background regions; and
(3) visual tokens emphasized at different attention stages encode information at varying levels of granularity, playing complementary roles within LVLMs.
Motivated by these insights, we propose HERO, a unified visual token early dropping framework for HR-LVLMs. By estimating tile importance from a multimodal perspective and retaining complementary visual tokens across local and global views, HERO effectively improves the computational efficiency of HR-LVLMs while maintaining strong performance. Our main contributions are concluded as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide empirical insights into three underexplored aspects of HR-LVLMs: (i) the estimation of tile importance, (ii) the layerwise dynamics of CLS attention in CLIP-based vision encoders, and (iii) the specialized roles of different visual tokens within LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose HERO, a visual token early dropping method that supports content-adaptive token budget allocation and function-aware token selection within HR-LVLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate HERO into representative HR-LVLMs of different model scales, outperforming existing methods across a wide range of tasks and benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Development of LVLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By integrating pretrained vision encoders with LLMs, researchers have developed LVLMs, which can jointly interpret visual and textual inputs. Most LVLMs employ a CLIP-ViT to obtain semantically rich visual features. However, CLIP-ViTs usually operate at fixed and limited input resolutions. This requires early LVLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> to downscale high-resolution images before encoding, which impairs their ability to capture fine-grained visual details. To address this limitation, HR-LVLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> are proposed. These models resize and partition the input image into local tiles, each matching the encoder’s input resolution, and simultaneously generate a downsized global thumbnail to preserve holistic context. All tiles and the thumbnail are encoded independently, and their features are concatenated in the sequence dimension before being passed to the LLM. While this strategy effectively preserves detailed visual information, it substantially increases the number of visual tokens, leading to significant computational overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Visual Token Compression in LVLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate computational overhead, visual token compression strategies are developed for LVLMs. Early approaches focus on designing novel adapters, such as Q-Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023a</a>)</cite> and C-Abstractor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cha et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, to distill visual features into a compact token set. However, these methods require model training, which increases deployment costs and hinders generalizability. To address these issues, inference-time token pruning methods are proposed. FastV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> and SparseVLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> rank visual tokens based on text-to-image attention within the LLM and discard those with low relevance. They avoid model training but can interfere with LLM inference mechanisms like KV caching and Flash-Attention. Therefore, early-dropping strategies are proposed to prune visual tokens before the LLM. PruMerge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and FasterVLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> utilize CLS attention from the vision encoder to discard uninformative visual tokens. However, they are designed for conventional LVLMs without considering the characteristics of HR-LVLMs. To address this, GlobalCo<math id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{m}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{m}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2025a</a>)</cite> and HiRED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arif et al. 2025</a>)</cite> are introduced. These methods first allocate token budgets across local tiles using the CLS attention from the global thumbnail, then select informative tokens within each tile based on local CLS attention. Although tailored for HR-LVLMs, they still exhibit limitations such as insufficient task relevance modeling, heuristic attention extraction, and uniform token selection, highlighting the need for further innovation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Key Observations</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the open questions left by prior methods, we conduct pilot studies focusing on three key aspects: (1) the measurement of tile importance, (2) the layerwise dynamics of CLS attention in CLIP-ViTs, and (3) specialized token selection for local tiles versus the global thumbnail.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Measurement of Tile Importance</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Estimating the relative importance across local tiles is crucial for allocating the limited token budget to the most informative regions. Existing methods address this by leveraging the CLS attention from the global thumbnail. However, this approach has two limitations: (1) it overlooks task relevance: regions that are not globally salient but are closely related to the instruction may be under-allocated; (2) it suffers from spatial misalignment: the thumbnail’s attention map may not precisely correspond to the patch grids of the local tiles.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure1.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:210/210;" width="210" height="210" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>CLS similarity to the global thumbnail and CLIP score with the instruction “How many crucifixes are there?” for each local tile. All scores are normalized across tiles.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that a robust tile importance estimator should integrate both visual and textual cues and be directly computable at the tile level. According to the properties of CLIP-ViTs, two metrics can be naturally obtained at the tile level: (1) the cosine similarity between each tile’s CLS token and that of the global thumbnail, which reflects regional visual saliency; and (2) the CLIP score between each tile and the textual instruction, which quantifies task relevance. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Measurement of Tile Importance ‣ 3 Key Observations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the CLS similarity highlights tiles containing prominent visual structures (e.g., the church body), while the CLIP score is more responsive to tiles aligned with the textual instruction (e.g., the two crucifixes on the roof), demonstrating their complementary strengths.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setting</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MME</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">POPE</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TextVQA</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RWQA</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">Global Thumbnail</th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">1490.9</th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">84.7</th>
<th id="S3.T1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">54.1</th>
