<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance of combining the global thumbnail with a single local tile. “+Top-<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>” denotes the global thumbnail plus the <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th ranked tile by CLS similarity or CLIP score, with no cumulative addition of tiles.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To quantitatively assess these metrics, we conduct experiments using LLaVA-NeXT-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> on four benchmarks of different categories: MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023b</a>)</cite>, TextVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al. 2019</a>)</cite> and RWQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI 2024</a>)</cite>. We first evaluate the model performance with only the global thumbnail. Then we observe the performance variation by adding a single local tile, where the tile is selected based on its ranking of CLS similarity or CLIP score. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Measurement of Tile Importance ‣ 3 Key Observations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, performance consistently improves as the most highly ranked tiles are included, whereas adding lower-ranked tiles may even reduce accuracy by introducing irrelevant or noisy information. These results validate the effectiveness of both metrics and suggest combining them for a more reliable estimation of tile importance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Dynamics of CLS Attention in CLIP-ViTs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In CLIP-ViTs, the CLS token aggregates global information, thereby its attention weights naturally indicate the relative importance of individual patch tokens. While existing methods leverage this property to guide token selection in each local tile and the global thumbnail, they typically rely on either the initial or final layers, overlooking intermediate layers that may encode richer and more diverse semantic cues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Dynamics of CLS Attention in CLIP-ViTs ‣ 3 Key Observations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the CLS attention exhibits a distinct two-stage pattern across layers 0–11 and 12–23. Additional visualizations provided in Appendix <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show that: in the first stage, CLS attention begins by focusing on edges and textures, and gradually shifts toward foreground objects; in contrast, the second stage shows a sharp transition, where attention becomes narrowly concentrated on a small subset of background patches. These observations lead to two insights: (1) the CLS attention in low-to-middle layers may be optimal for capturing the main semantic content, rather than that in the initial or final layers; (2) the optimal attention layer varies across image types, indicating that relying on a single layer is inadequate for robust token selection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:245/209;" width="245" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Cosine similarity between CLS attention maps from different layers of CLIP-ViT-L/336px. The similarities are averaged across all attention heads and obtained using all images in the MME benchmark.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
