</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Roles of Different Visual Tokens in LVLMs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As revealed in Section 3.2, the CLS token attends to different types of patch tokens across layers: in low-to-middle layers, it predominantly focuses on tokens that are spatially aligned with main objects (i.e., object tokens), whereas in middle-to-high layers, its attention shifts toward a small subset of tokens located in background regions (i.e., artifact tokens).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While prior work has demonstrated the utility of artifact tokens in preserving global information for image classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Darcet et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, we extend this investigation to LVLMs. We employ LLaVA-1.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> to generate captions for each image in the MME benchmark with two inference settings: (1) retain the top 10% most-attended tokens from layers 4–8, and (2) retain the top 10% most-attended tokens from layers 12-23. The generated captions are evaluated by GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024</a>)</cite> along four dimensions: object-level coverage, object-level correctness, global-level coverage, and global-level correctness (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Evaluation Protocol for Image Captioning ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for detailed evaluation protocol). As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Dynamics of CLS Attention in CLIP-ViTs ‣ 3 Key Observations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, retaining object tokens yields higher scores in object-level metrics, reflecting stronger fine-grained detail capture. In contrast, retaining artifact tokens leads to better global-level performance, underscoring their role in scene-level perception. These findings suggest a functional division: object tokens are best retained in local tiles to preserve fine-grained semantics, while artifact tokens are better retained in the global thumbnail to convey holistic context.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by these findings, we propose HERO, a visual token early dropping framework tailored for HR-LVLMs. Below, we first review the baseline model, then detail the design of HERO, and finally analyze its computational savings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Baseline Model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt LLaVA-NeXT as the baseline HR-LVLM for both its representativeness and widespread use in related studies.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Model Architecture</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLaVA-NeXT follows the standard LVLM architecture, employing CLIP-ViT-L/336px as the vision encoder, a two-layer MLP as the vision-language adapter, and Vicuna-v1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al. 2023</a>)</cite> as the LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Inference Pipeline</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLaVA-NeXT adopts an image cropping strategy to preserve visual details in high-resolution images. It defines five target resolutions: <math id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{</annotation></semantics></math>(336, 672), (672, 336), (672, 672), (1008, 336), (336, 1008)<math id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to five tiling grids: <math id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{</annotation></semantics></math>(1, 2), (2, 1), (2, 2), (3, 1), (1, 3)<math id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each input image, it selects the target resolution with the closest aspect ratio. The image is first padded along the shorter side to match the aspect ratio and then resized to the target resolution. The resulting image is divided into non-overlapping tiles, each of size <math id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="336^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>336</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">336^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the input resolution of the vision encoder. Meanwhile, the original image is resized to <math id="S4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="336^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>336</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">336^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a global thumbnail that retains holistic context. Both the local tiles and the global thumbnail are independently encoded by the vision encoder, each yielding 576 patch features and one CLS feature. The patch features are projected through the MLP adapter to generate visual tokens. Finally, these visual tokens are concatenated in sequence with the instruction embeddings and fed into the LLM for multimodal understanding and text generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Design of HERO</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Dynamics of CLS Attention in CLIP-ViTs ‣ 3 Key Observations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, HERO has a two-stage workflow. First, it adaptively allocates a user-defined token budget across local tiles and the global thumbnail. Then, it selects a specified number of tokens to retain within each region based on CLS attention extracted from different layers of the vision encoder. HERO is training-free and can be seamlessly integrated into mainstream HR-LVLM architectures.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Token Budgeting</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To accommodate different computational constraints, HERO allows users to specify a target visual token retention ratio <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Given that an input image is divided into <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> local tiles and one global thumbnail, each producing <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> visual tokens, the total budget of visual tokens is computed as <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}=\lfloor(K+1)\cdot N\cdot R\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}=\lfloor(K+1)\cdot N\cdot R\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. A fixed quota of <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}=\lfloor N\cdot R\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}=\lfloor N\cdot R\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> tokens is first reserved for the global thumbnail, while the remaining tokens are distributed among the local tiles in a content-adaptive manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by Section 3.1, HERO estimates tile importance by jointly considering the regional visual saliency and task relevance. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLSsim}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CLSsim</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLSsim}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the cosine similarity between the CLS token of tile <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and that of the global thumbnail. The visual saliency score for each tile is computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{v}_{i}=\frac{\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLSsim}_{i}/T_{v})}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLSsim}_{j}/T_{v})},\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CLSsim</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CLSsim</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{v}_{i}=\frac{\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLSsim}_{i}/T_{v})}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLSsim}_{j}/T_{v})},\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature coefficient that controls the sharpness of the score distribution. For task relevance, HERO leverages the CLIP score between each tile and the textual instruction. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CLIPscore}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CLIPscore</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CLIPscore}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the CLIP score for tile <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The task relevance score for each tile is computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{t}_{i}=\frac{\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLIPscore}_{i}/T_{t})}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLIPscore}_{j}/T_{t})},\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CLIPscore</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CLIPscore</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{t}_{i}=\frac{\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLIPscore}_{i}/T_{t})}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{CLIPscore}_{j}/T_{t})},\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is similarly used to control the sharpness of the score distribution. The overall importance of each tile is then computed by linearly combining the two scores:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{i}=\alpha\cdot s^{v}_{i}+(1-\alpha)\cdot s^{t}_{i},\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\alpha\cdot s^{v}_{i}+(1-\alpha)\cdot s^{t}_{i},\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is used to control the balance between visual and textual signals. Finally, the token budget assigned to each tile is determined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{L_{i}}=\lfloor K\cdot N\cdot R\cdot s_{i}\rfloor,\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L_{i}}=\lfloor K\cdot N\cdot R\cdot s_{i}\rfloor,\quad i\in\{1,2,..K\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">This entire process is conducted at the tile level, integrating both vision-guided and text-guided signals to achieve efficient and adaptive token budget allocation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Token Selection</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once token budgets are allocated, HERO selects the specified number of most informative tokens to retain within each tile and the global thumbnail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Sections 3.2 and 3.3, CLS attention in the vision encoder highlights different visual tokens across layers: in low-to-middle layers, the CLS token primarily attends to object tokens that convey fine-grained semantics, while in high layers, its focus shifts toward artifact tokens that encode global context. To leverage this behavior, HERO defines two sets of layer indices, <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> for low-level layers and <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> for high-level layers, and employs CLS attention from these sets to guide token selection in a function-aware manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the global thumbnail, which serves to provide scene-level semantics, HERO computes token importance by averaging CLS attention across the layers in <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{G}_{j}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{H}}a_{j}^{G,l},\quad j\in\{1,2,..,N\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{G}_{j}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{H}}a_{j}^{G,l},\quad j\in\{1,2,..,N\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{G,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{G,l}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the attention weight from the CLS token to patch token <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in layer <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, averaged across all attention heads. The top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens within the global thumbnail are retained based on these scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For each local tile, which aims to preserve fine-grained visual details, token importance is estimated by averaging CLS attention across the layers in <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{j}^{L_{i}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{L}|}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}a_{j}^{L_{i},l},\quad j\in\{1,2,..N\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{L_{i}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{L}|}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}a_{j}^{L_{i},l},\quad j\in\{1,2,..N\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{L_{i},l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{L_{i},l}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the CLS attention weight to patch token <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in tile <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at layer <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, averaged across all attention heads. The top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens within tile <math id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> are retained accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This token selection strategy provides two advantages. First, it applies distinct selection criteria to global and local views, aligning with their complementary roles. Second, aggregating CLS attention across multiple layers improves robustness and adaptability to diverse visual inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Theoretical Efficiency Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In LVLMs, the LLM constitutes the primary source of computational overhead. During inference, the LLM processes all input tokens in a single forward pass (known as the prefill stage) to cache intermediate states for subsequent decoding. This stage requires every input token to participate in self-attention computation, thus dominating the overall computational complexity. Specifically, the per-layer FLOPs of the LLM during the prefill stage can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8(N_{v}+N_{t})d^{2}+4(N_{v}+N_{t})^{2}d+6(N_{v}+N_{t})dm," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8(N_{v}+N_{t})d^{2}+4(N_{v}+N_{t})^{2}d+6(N_{v}+N_{t})dm,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of visual and textual tokens, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden dimension of the LLM, and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the intermediate dimension of its MLP layers. As <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> typically dominates <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, visual tokens constitute the main computational bottleneck. By applying HERO with a token retention ratio <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the per-layer FLOPs get updated to:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8(RN_{v}+N_{t})d^{2}+4(RN_{v}+N_{t})^{2}d+6(RN_{v}+N_{t})dm." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8(RN_{v}+N_{t})d^{2}+4(RN_{v}+N_{t})^{2}d+6(RN_{v}+N_{t})dm.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In typical scenarios where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}\gg N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}\gg N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RN_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RN_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to a substantial drop in the quadratic term, yielding significant improvements in computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
