</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setting</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate HERO with both LLaVA-NeXT-7B and LLaVA-NeXT-13B. For token budget allocation, we set <math id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}=0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}=0.03</annotation></semantics></math>. For token selection, we set <math id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\{5,6,7,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\{5,6,7,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{21\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{21\}</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted on 8 NVIDIA L20 GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Benchmarks</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct evaluations on ten benchmarks covering diverse tasks: VQAv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, VizWiz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurari et al. 2018</a>)</cite>, and GQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hudson and Manning 2019</a>)</cite> for general visual understanding; SQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> and ChartQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masry et al. 2022</a>)</cite> for multimodal reasoning; TextVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al. 2019</a>)</cite> and OCRBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite> for text recognition; MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and MMB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2025b</a>)</cite> for comprehensive evaluation; and POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023b</a>)</cite> for hallucination assessment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Comparison Methods</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare HERO with three categories of visual token compression methods: (1) methods that drop visual tokens within the LLM: FastV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> and SparseVLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>; (2) methods that apply early dropping but are not tailored for HR-LVLMs: LLaVA-Prumerge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and FasterVLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>; (3) methods that apply early dropping and explicitly designed for HR-LVLMs: HiRED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arif et al. 2025</a>)</cite> and GlobalCo<math id="S5.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{m}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{m}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate HERO under two representative token budget settings: a moderate one (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and an aggressive one (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">With LLaVA-NeXT-7B</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results on LLaVA-NeXT-7B are shown in the upper section of Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Theoretical Efficiency Analysis ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Under the moderate budget setting, although we adopt the lowest token retention ratio among all compared methods, HERO still achieves the best performance on 7 out of 10 benchmarks. On the remaining benchmarks, it consistently ranks second, closely trailing the best-performing methods. Notably, HERO even outperforms the full-token baseline on OCRBench, MME, MMB and POPE, likely due to the removal of noisy or unrelated visual information. Under the aggressive budget setting, HERO continues to deliver strong results. It attains the best performance on 8 out of 10 benchmarks and respectively ranks second and third on the remaining two. These results underscore HERO’s effectiveness in balancing computational efficiency and task performance for HR-LVLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">With LLaVA-NeXT-13B</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results on LLaVA-NeXT-13B are presented in the lower section of Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Theoretical Efficiency Analysis ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Under the moderate budget setting, HERO outperforms all competing methods on 9 out of 10 benchmarks and secures the second-best score on the remaining one. Furthermore, HERO surpasses the full-token baseline on OCRBench and POPE, highlighting its capacity to retain the critical visual information. Under the aggressive budget setting, HERO achieves the best performance on 8 benchmarks and ranks second on the remaining two. These results further confirm HERO’s robustness and effectiveness across different model scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct ablation studies in two parts: (1) evaluating the contribution of each key component, and (2) identifying the optimal configuration for each hyperparameter.</p>
</div>
