<section id="S5.SS3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Component Ablation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the contribution of each component by progressively removing them. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Component Ablation ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, removing task relevance during budget allocation causes an immediate performance decline, underscoring the importance of textual guidance. Further eliminating visual saliency and adopting uniform token budgets results in additional performance degradation, especially on SQA, highlighting the importance of adaptive budget allocation. Replacing multi-layer attention aggregation in <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> with a single-layer approach also impairs results, confirming the robustness gained from multi-layer attention utilization. Finally, treating tiles and the global thumbnail identically during token selection yields the worst results, demonstrating the necessity of function-aware token selection.</p>
</div>
