</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Ablation results of progressively removing key components with <math id="S5.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=40\%</annotation></semantics></math>. “A” denotes the task relevance score, “B” denotes the visual saliency score, “C” denotes the multi-layer attention utilization, “D” denotes the differentiated token selection for local tiles and global thumbnail.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Hyperparameter Ablation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first ablate values of <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which balances visual saliency and task relevance during tile importance estimation in Eq.<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Token Budgeting ‣ 4.2 Design of HERO ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. As shown in Fig.<a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Hyperparameter Ablation ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, both MME and POPE exhibit an inverted-U trend as <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases from 0 to 1, with performance peaking at <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. TextVQA also achieves relatively strong performance at <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, but its accuracy declines as visual saliency becomes overly dominant. In contrast, SQA benefits from stronger visual guidance, showing continuous improvement as <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases and reaching its maximum at <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Overall, <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> delivers the most robust and stable performance across the benchmarks, justifying its choice as our default setting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13067v1/figure4.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:245/155;" width="245" height="155"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Ablation results of <math id="S5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=25\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We next ablate values of <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which control the sharpness of visual saliency and task relevance score distributions in Eq.<a href="#S4.E1" title="In Token Budgeting ‣ 4.2 Design of HERO ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Eq.<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Token Budgeting ‣ 4.2 Design of HERO ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We evaluate all combinations of <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with values in <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{</annotation></semantics></math>0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07, 0.09<math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\}</annotation></semantics></math> on the MME benchmark. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Hyperparameter Ablation ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the performance exhibits non-monotonic trends as either temperature is varied, indicating that both under- and over-smoothing can harm downstream results. Among all combinations, the configuration <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{v}=0.05,T_{t}=0.03)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{v}=0.05,T_{t}=0.03)</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the highest performance retention, with other settings generally leading to lower scores. We thus adopt it as the default setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we ablate layer selections for <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, which guide token selection for local tiles and the global thumbnail in Eq.<a href="#S4.E5" title="In Token Selection ‣ 4.2 Design of HERO ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Eq.<a href="#S4.E6" title="In Token Selection ‣ 4.2 Design of HERO ‣ 4 Method ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix A Ablation Results on Layer Selection ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> of Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Ablation Results on Layer Selection ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, the configuration <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\{5,6,7,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\{5,6,7,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{21\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{21\}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the best overall performance. Broadening <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to earlier layers or narrowing it to fewer layers both degrades performance, suggesting that layers 5 to 8 capture the most informative fine-grained information. Interestingly, extending <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> to a broader set results in performance drops, indicating that layer 21 strikes the best balance between abstraction and specificity for global context. These results support our layer settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure5.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:210/155;" width="210" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Ablation results of <math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=25\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Practical Efficiency Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the real-world efficiency gains provided by HERO, we evaluate three inference settings of LLaVA-NeXT-7B on the MME benchmark: (1) using 100% visual tokens, (2) retaining 40% visual tokens with HERO, and (3) retaining 20% visual tokens with HERO. For each setting, we compare the total number of input tokens, TFLOPs, GPU memory, KV-cache, throughput, and model performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, HERO substantially reduces the number of input tokens to the LLM, resulting in significant computational and memory savings. Specifically, compared to the full-token baseline, TFLOPs are respectively reduced from 38.4 to 14.1 and 7.7 for the 40% and 20% budget settings. GPU memory usage drops from 21.1 GB to 17.0 GB and 16.7 GB, while KV-cache decreases from 1336.5 MB to 552.5 MB and 291.5 MB. These resource optimizations directly translate to faster inference: generation throughput increases from 9.6 tokens/s to 15.6 tokens/s and 22.8 tokens/s as the visual token count is reduced. Importantly, these efficiency gains come with minimal or no accuracy loss. HERO even surpasses the full-token baseline while removing 60% of visual tokens. These results demonstrate HERO’s practicality for efficient deployment of HR-LVLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by insights into tile importance estimation, CLS attention dynamics, and specialized roles of visual tokens, we propose HERO, an inference-time visual token pruning strategy that effectively improves the computational efficiency of HR-LVLMs with minimal performance degradation. A potential limitation of HERO lies in its reliance on manually selected hyperparameters. While the default settings perform well on public benchmarks, the optimal values may vary across tasks. Future work will explore incorporating learnable hyperparameters to further enhance the adaptability and performance of HERO.</p>
</div>
</section>
