<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Ablation results on layer selections in <math id="A1.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. “Low Layers” denote the configuration for <math id="A1.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, while “High Layers” denote the configuration for <math id="A1.T6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted with <math id="A1.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=25\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Evaluation Protocol for Image Captioning</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the distinct roles of object tokens and artifact tokens in LVLMs, we design an image captioning experiment to isolate and compare the sematic information captured by these two types of visual tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each image in the MME benchmark, we generate two versions of image captions using LLaVA-1.5-7B under two inference settings:(1) retaining only the top 10% most-attended patch tokens from layers 4-8 (designated as object tokens), and (2) retaining only the top 10% most-attended patch tokens from layers 12-23 (designated as artifact tokens). We employ the MME benchmark as it offers a moderate size for manageable evaluation costs, while remaining sufficiently diverse to ensure representativeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the need for consistent and objective evaluation, we employ the commercial LVLM GPT-4o as an automatic evaluator (API version: gpt-4o-2024-08-06). For each image, GPT-4o is prompted to compare the two generated captions against the image content and score them along four predefined dimensions, as shown in Fig.<a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix B Evaluation Protocol for Image Captioning ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. All evaluations are conducted in a double-blind manner.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure6.png" id="A2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:279/413;" width="279" height="413" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The prompt provided to GPT-4o for conducting image caption evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure7.png" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/339;" width="628" height="339" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Qualitative examples of HERO’s token pruning under a 20% visual token budget. In each example, unmasked regions indicate the retained visual tokens, with the corresponding instructions and LVLM responses displayed on the right.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Token Pruning Case Study</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To qualitatively assess the effectiveness of HERO, we visualize its token pruning results on several instruction-image pairs with a visual token retention ratio of 20% , as shown in Fig.<a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix B Evaluation Protocol for Image Captioning ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. In each example, retained visual tokens are displayed as unmasked patches, while pruned visual tokens are masked in red. All experiments are conducted using LLaVA-NeXT-7B as the baseline HR-LVLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated, HERO consistently preserves both task-relevant regions (those closely aligned with the instruction) and visually salient areas (those important based on the image content itself). For instance, when the instruction asks about the brand of a car, tokens covering the car are largely retained, while uninformative background regions are pruned. When the instruction concerns counting painted rabbits or reading specific text, HERO maintains tokens corresponding to these semantic objects or text areas, even if they are spatially scattered or occupy a small portion of the image. Similarly, for questions about rare objects (e.g., a white tank), HERO successfully preserves the corresponding tokens despite the presence of surrounding visual clutter.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These visualizations demonstrate that HERO not only reduces the number of visual tokens, but does so in an instruction-aware and context-sensitive manner, effectively preserving the most critical information for visual understanding and downstream reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Supplementary Attention Visualizations</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further interpret the two-stage CLS attention pattern observed in the similarity heatmap (see Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Dynamics of CLS Attention in CLIP-ViTs ‣ 3 Key Observations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in Section 3.1), we provide detailed visualizations of CLS attention across all layers of the CLIP-ViT model on representative image examples. These visualizations aim to elucidate how CLS attention evolves throughout the network under different types of visual inputs, especially in light of the distinct behaviors observed between lower and higher layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We select four diverse images as case studies: (1) Fig.<a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows a simple real-world image containing a single foreground object (a cake on a plate); (2) Fig.<a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> presents a real-world image containing a small number of foreground objects (two people by a lake); (3) Fig.<a href="#A4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> depicts a cluttered street scene with multiple objects and rich background detail; (4) Fig.<a href="#A4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> illustrates a text-intensive document image. These examples are chosen to span a wide range of semantic density and spatial layout complexity, enabling a broad characterization of CLS attention dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across all cases, we observe a consistent layerwise progression of CLS attention:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initial layers (Layers 0–1):</span> CLS attention distributes broadly across the image, primarily focusing on low-level features such as edges, textures, and local patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-to-Middle layers (Layers 2–8):</span> CLS attention gradually consolidates onto semantically meaningful objects or regions within the image.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transition phase (Layers 9–12):</span>
CLS attention begins to shift focus away from the semantically meaningful regions, increasingly attending to background patches. This transition is particularly evident around layer 12, aligning with the phase shift observed in Section 3.1.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High layers (Layers 13–20):</span> CLS attention stabilizes and becomes sharply concentrated on a small subset of tokens, typically associated with background or shortcut cues rather than the primary semantic content.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final layers (Layers 21–23):</span> CLS attention exhibits mild diffusion, but the overall focus remains concentrated on the background tokens identified in preceding layers.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While this overall trend is consistent, the detailed progression of CLS attention varies across image types. In Fig.<a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, which depicts a single object against a clean background, the CLS token rapidly locks onto the cake within the early layers. A similar pattern is observed in Fig.<a href="#A4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, where attention focuses on the key text regions from the very beginning, likely due to the high-contrast edges and linear structures of textual content. In contrast, Fig.<a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and Fig.<a href="#A4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Attention Visualizations ‣ HERO: Rethinking Visual Token Early Dropping in High-Resolution Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> require more layers for the CLS token to comprehensively attend to the multiple or spatially dispersed foreground objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These observations offer two practical insights. First, CLS attention from low-to-middle layers is most suitable for identifying tokens associated with critical visual content, especially in complex scenes. This stands in contrast to prior studies that rely solely on the first or last layer, where attention may be either overly diffuse on low-level patterns or biased toward background artifacts. Second, since the attention trajectory varies with image type, aggregating attention across multiple low-to-middle layers improves robustness by ensuring that informative tokens are consistently captured across diverse visual inputs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure8.png" id="A4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:600/414;" width="600" height="414" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Layerwise progression of CLS attention on an image containing a single object.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure9.png" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:600/411;" width="600" height="411" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Layerwise progression of CLS attention on an image containing a few objects.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13067v1/figure10.png" id="A4.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:600/411;" width="600" height="411" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Layerwise progression of CLS attention on a complex scene.</figcaption>
</figure>
